Multi-Layer Biological Rejuvenation System (MLBRS v3.0): A Sequenced, Multi‑Modal Gerotherapeutic Framework Integrating Epigenetic Reprogramming, Immune Rejuvenation, Cellular Senescence Clearance, Microbiome Modulation, and Generative AI‑Guided Adaptive Control for Healthspan Extension

A Theoretical Systems Geroscience Doctoral Thesis

ouadi Maakoul & All smart LLM

Preamble

This doctoral thesis advances a theoretical framework for precision geromedicine. All proposals are grounded exclusively in published, peer‑reviewed, or publicly disclosed research current to May 2026; no original laboratory experimentation by the author is presented. The central contribution is the integration of five recent geroscience breakthroughs into a physiologically rational, staged, and computationally orchestrated clinical architecture. The validation process, conducted through systematic literature review and critical appraisal, has generated a set of explicit preclinical triggers, safety thresholds, and methodological refinements—presented herein—that transform the original outline into a testable, ethically defensible, and fundable research programme.

---

Abstract

Human ageing proceeds through the progressive, interdependent deterioration of twelve molecular, cellular, and systemic hallmarks. No single therapeutic modality can simultaneously address genomic instability, epigenetic drift, cellular senescence, immunosenescence, mitochondrial dysfunction, and dysbiosis. The Multi‑Layer Biological Rejuvenation System v3.0 (MLBRS v3.0) articulates a two‑phase gerotherapeutic protocol: Phase 1—Restorative Medicine—establishes systemic homeostasis through butyrate‑mediated senomorphic suppression of the senescence‑associated secretory phenotype (SASP), transient liver‑directed mRNA delivery of DLL1/FLT3L/IL‑7 for thymopoiesis restoration, and anti‑uPAR CAR‑T‑mediated senescent‑cell clearance, with a contingency anti‑NKG2D CAR‑T track. Phase 2—Regenerative Medicine—applies reduced‑factor (OSK) doxycycline‑inducible partial epigenetic reprogramming and structural regeneration within the permissive, inflammation‑resolved host created by Phase 1. An Adaptive Control Layer combines AURORA‑style generative reconstruction with counterfactual reasoning for digital‑twin forecasting, LinAge2‑based biological‑age monitoring, and an AI‑guided dosing optimiser with built‑in out‑of‑distribution safeguards.

The thesis incorporates a comprehensive preclinical validation programme that directly addresses the risk of inter‑layer antagonism through organoid and aged‑animal screening, defines quantitative entry criteria for the regenerative phase, establishes explicit CAR‑T track‑pivot rules and teratoma surveillance protocols, embeds polypharmacy‑stratified enrolment and immunogenicity mitigation, and replaces aspirational timelines with milestones contingent on demonstrated readiness. A refined challenge–solution matrix captures additional failure modes—cytokine release syndrome, anti‑drug antibodies, and long‑term ecological disruption—and maps them to verified mitigation strategies. The resulting framework constitutes a disciplined, staged, and computationally informed blueprint for the coming decade of precision geromedicine.

