1. Introduction: The Multi‑Layer Challenge of Human Ageing

The 2023 expansion of the hallmarks of ageing framework (López‑Otín et al., 2023) enumerates twelve inter‑connected drivers: genomic instability, telomere attrition, epigenetic alterations, loss of proteostasis, disabled macroautophagy, deregulated nutrient sensing, mitochondrial dysfunction, cellular senescence, stem‑cell exhaustion, altered intercellular communication, chronic inflammation, and dysbiosis. In parallel, immunosenescence has been codified into eleven molecular hallmarks that collectively degrade adaptive immune competence (translational neurodegeneration literature, 2025). These hallmarks do not operate in isolation; they form a densely coupled network in which degradation in any single layer accelerates decline in others through feed‑forward inflammatory and metabolic loops.

The structural clinical problem is that no monotherapy can address all hallmarks simultaneously. A senolytic administered in isolation cannot correct the epigenetic noise that predisposes cells to senescence; partial epigenetic reprogramming cannot clear pre‑existing senescent cells or resolve the inflammaging milieu that impairs regeneration; and none of these interventions can operate optimally in a host whose thymus has involuted and whose T‑cell repertoire has collapsed. The field has responded by establishing dedicated multimodal trial centres—most notably the NUS Academy for Healthy Longevity Clinical Trial Centre, which opened in October 2025 and is currently running the PROMETHEUS precision geromedicine protocol. Yet what the field lacks is a unified, physiologically sequenced architecture that respects the dependencies between biological layers: specifically, the principle that regenerative interventions should be deployed only after the restoration of systemic homeostasis.

The period 2025–2026 brought five breakthroughs that, in combination, make such an architecture credible: (i) the FDA clearance of ER‑100, the first clinical partial‑reprogramming therapy; (ii) the demonstration of transient, liver‑directed mRNA‑mediated restoration of thymopoiesis; (iii) the validation of anti‑uPAR and anti‑NKG2D CAR‑T cells as senescence‑clearing agents; (iv) the characterisation of butyrate as a senomorphic metabolite; and (v) the publication of the AURORA generative AI platform for multi‑omic reconstruction and in silico perturbation. This thesis organises these components into the Multi‑Layer Biological Rejuvenation System v3.0 (MLBRS v3.0), subjects the integration to a rigorous literature‑anchored challenge–solution analysis, and defines the preclinical validation and safety infrastructure required before human translation.

