2. Central Hypothesis

Ageing is a partially modifiable, multi‑layer network phenomenon that can be functionally attenuated through a physiologically sequenced, two‑phase gerotherapeutic protocol. Phase 1 restores systemic homeostasis (inflammatory resolution, immune competence, metabolic stabilisation); Phase 2 regenerates tissue structure and epigenetic identity within this permissive environment. The entire protocol is orchestrated by a generative AI platform that reconstructs the individual multi‑omic ageing trajectory and forecasts intervention response, while a parallel counterfactual layer estimates individual‑level treatment effects.

The hypothesis is falsifiable at each layer. Phase 1 interventions must produce measurable reductions in circulating inflammatory biomarkers (IL‑6, IL‑8, TNF‑α), a clinically meaningful deceleration in LinAge2‑quantified biological age, and evidence of renewed thymic output. Phase 2 interventions, when deployed against this background, must yield functional gains (grip strength, VO₂ max, cognitive performance) superior to those achieved by Phase 2 administered without Phase 1 preconditioning. Failure of Phase 1 to meet pre‑specified biomarker thresholds halts progression to Phase 2 and triggers protocol revision.

