3. Architecture: The Two‑Phase Clinical Framework

3.1 Phase 1—Restorative Medicine: Clearing the Rubble

The objective of Phase 1 is to eliminate accumulated molecular and cellular damage, resolve chronic systemic inflammation, and restore adaptive immune competence. The phase is subdivided into three subphases, deployed in a toxicity‑minimising, physiology‑respecting sequence.

3.1.1 Microbiome–Epigenetic Layer: Butyrate as a Senomorphic Agent

The gut microbiome exerts systemic immunomodulatory effects through short‑chain fatty acid metabolites. Butyrate, in particular, has been definitively characterised as a senomorphic agent: it suppresses the induction of a senescence phenotype in aged T cells and inhibits secretion of pro‑inflammatory SASP factors including IL‑6 and IL‑8 (Rees et al., 2025). Mechanistically, butyrate inhibits histone deacetylases, reduces mTOR‑dependent NF‑κB activation, and attenuates mitochondrial reactive oxygen species accumulation.

This layer is deployed first (Subphase 1A) because it represents the lowest‑risk entry point—an orally bioavailable, GRAS‑status postbiotic. However, the critical limitation of oral butyrate is its extremely short plasma half‑life and rapid colonic metabolism, which makes achieving sustained systemic concentrations pharmacokinetically challenging. The MLBRS protocol therefore incorporates a pre‑specified biomarker‑based escalation rule: if IL‑6 reduction is <20% from baseline at Week 4 despite optimised butyrate delivery (including enteric‑coated formulations or tributyrin prodrugs), the participant proceeds to Subphase 1B while continuing butyrate, with the recognition that its contribution may be modest. This rule prevents indefinite delay of the higher‑potency immunorestorative and senolytic layers.

3.1.2 Immuno‑Restorative Layer: Liver‑Directed mRNA Reconstitution of Thymopoiesis

Immunosenescence is driven primarily by thymic involution. Friedrich et al. (2025) demonstrated that delivery of mRNAs encoding Delta‑like ligand 1 (DLL1), FLT3L, and IL‑7 to hepatocytes via lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) transiently repurposes the liver as a trophic‑factor factory, expanding common lymphoid progenitors and replenishing peripheral T‑cell compartments in aged mice. Critically, mRNA‑encoded delivery produced substantially lower peak serum cytokine levels than recombinant protein administration, an essential safety feature for older adults.

To mitigate the recognised risk of hepatic off‑target expression and associated CD8⁺ T‑cell suppression, the mRNA constructs are engineered with one to five copies of miR‑122 binding sites in the 3′ untranslated region. miR‑122 is a liver‑specific microRNA; its target sites significantly reduce hepatic expression while preserving efficacy in extrahepatic tissues (Barry et al., 2024).

Subphase 1B is initiated only when the host meets explicit immunological entry criteria intended to ensure a permissive inflammatory environment: (a) circulating IL‑6 <2 pg/mL and TNF‑α <1.5 pg/mL, (b) C‑reactive protein <1 mg/L, and (c) neutrophil‑to‑lymphocyte ratio <3. These values are derived from young‑adult reference ranges and from cohorts in which thymopoiesis restoration was demonstrated in preclinical models.

3.1.3 Cellular Clearance Layer: CAR‑T‑Mediated Senescent‑Cell Elimination

Senescent cells accumulate with age and secrete a pro‑inflammatory SASP that disrupts tissue homeostasis. Small‑molecule senolytics face limitations of systemic toxicity (e.g., navitoclax‑induced thrombocytopenia) and emerging resistance. Immune‑based senolysis offers an orthogonal strategy: anti‑uPAR CAR‑T cells administered to aged mice improved intestinal barrier function, regenerative capacity, and mucosal immune function, with a single dose reversing ageing‑associated deficits (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, 2025). However, uPAR is not a universal senescence marker; a 2025 review in Frontiers in Immunology identifies anti‑NKG2D CAR‑T cells as an alternative that targets senescent cells in brain, skeletal muscle, and subcutaneous fat without haematological abnormalities.

The MLBRS protocol adopts an explicit two‑track design with pre‑specified pivot criteria, replacing the earlier aspirational language with objective, measurable thresholds:

· Track A (primary): Anti‑uPAR CAR‑T cells.
· Track B (contingency): Anti‑NKG2D CAR‑T cells, activated if any of the following criteria are met at the 3‑month surveillance biopsy:
    (i) >10% stromal/endothelial CAR‑T infiltration in intestinal tissue,
    (ii) serum ALT/AST increase >3‑fold above baseline,
    (iii) circulating endothelial cell count >50% above baseline.

This layer is deployed as Subphase 1C, after T‑cell reconstitution, to ensure that the host possesses a competent endogenous immune system capable of cooperating with the infused CAR‑T product.

3.1.4 Safety Architecture for Engineered Cells

All CAR‑T products and any subsequent engineered vectors deployed in Phase 2 incorporate an iCasp9/AP1903 suicide‑gene safety switch. The iCasp9 system consists of full‑length caspase 9 fused to a modified FKBP12 domain; administration of the otherwise inert small‑molecule dimerizer AP1903 rapidly induces apoptosis, eliminating >90% of engineered cells (clinical validations in CAR‑T oncology programmes, 2025). This is augmented by three additional safety layers identified in the validation phase of this thesis:

· Immunogenicity monitoring and mitigation: Anti‑PEG antibodies against LNP‑delivered mRNA and anti‑drug antibodies against CAR‑T scFv components will be measured monthly. Pre‑specified protocols for tocilizumab (IL‑6 receptor blockade) are on standby for cytokine release syndrome (CRS) management, consistent with emerging practice in older oncology populations.
· Long‑term ecological surveillance: Because senescent cells play beneficial roles in wound healing and tumour suppression, a dedicated surveillance plan monitors wound‑healing rates (standardised skin biopsy closure), annual cancer incidence, and frailty index trajectories for 5 years post‑infusion.
· Microbiome rescue strategy: Metagenomic monitoring is performed quarterly; if dysbiosis indices exceed pre‑specified thresholds, faecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) from baseline autologous or healthy donor material is protocolised as a rescue intervention.

3.1.5 Metabolic Optimisation Layer

Carried forward from earlier MLBRS iterations, this layer addresses deregulated nutrient sensing and mitochondrial dysfunction through modulation of mTOR/AMPK signalling, NAD⁺ restoration, and individualised exercise and nutritional protocols. Its placement in Phase 1 ensures metabolic competence before the high‑energy demands of tissue regeneration in Phase 2.

3.2 Phase 2—Regenerative Medicine: Rebuilding Structure and Identity

Phase 2 is initiated only when the host has demonstrated that Phase 1 has achieved its restorative objectives. The previously vague entry criteria are now replaced by a quantitative, seven‑marker panel and a partial‑response decision pathway.

