3.2.2 Epigenetic Reprogramming Layer

The FDA‑cleared ER‑100 trial (Life Biosciences, January 2026) validated the safety rationale for reduced‑factor (OSK, omitting c‑MYC) partial reprogramming under doxycycline‑inducible control. The MLBRS protocol is ER‑100‑informed but adapted for systemic application. The core innovation lies in the comprehensive teratoma surveillance and safety validation programme that must be completed in the preclinical phase of this doctoral programme before any human Phase 2 initiation:

· Pre‑clinical teratoma screening (Years 2–3): A 24‑week study in aged mice with doxycycline‑inducible OSK expression, randomised to OSK + iCasp9, OSK alone, or iCasp9 alone. Endpoints include histopathological teratoma formation, OSK expression kinetics, and iCasp9 activation efficiency. The success criterion is zero teratomas in the OSK + iCasp9 arm with teratomas in the OSK‑only arm confirming model sensitivity.
· Clinical teratoma surveillance (Phase 2): Baseline whole‑body MRI; monthly clinical examination for masses and organ enlargement; quarterly organ‑specific imaging (abdominal ultrasound, chest X‑ray, brain MRI); annual whole‑body MRI. Any suspicious finding triggers urgent biopsy and, if teratoma is confirmed, immediate AP1903 administration (0.4 mg/kg IV) with repeat imaging at 72 hours and 1 week to document regression.

3.2.3 Structural and Neuroregenerative Layers

Mesenchymal stem‑cell therapy, smart biomaterials, and 3D bioprinting of skeletal tissues are deployed within the non‑inflammatory host environment created by Phase 1. The neuroregenerative module couples focused‑ultrasound‑mediated blood–brain barrier opening with targeted neurotrophic factor delivery (BDNF, GDNF) and AI‑guided cognitive training. This module remains the least mechanistically mature and is designated a priority for foundational investigation within the doctoral programme.

