4. The Adaptive Control Layer: Generative Forecasting, Not Causal Inference

4.1 The AURORA Generative Reconstruction Paradigm

Published in Cell Metabolism in April 2026, the AURORA framework integrates seven modalities—transcriptomics, metabolomics, microbiome, 3D facial imaging, thermal imaging, and clinical laboratories—across 425,258 individuals and 581,763 samples. It harmonises batch effects, imputes missing modalities, builds multimodal ageing clocks, and performs in silico perturbation analysis.

AURORA is a generative reconstruction and forecasting engine, not a causal inference tool. The MLBRS Adaptive Control Layer therefore adopts a two‑tier architecture:

· Tier 1: AURORA‑based reconstruction for digital‑twin forecasting and missing‑omic imputation.
· Tier 2: Formal counterfactual estimation using DoWhy and EconML to estimate individual‑level treatment effects under alternative intervention regimes.

An out‑of‑distribution (OOD) detection module is integrated: when the participant’s state vector falls outside the AURORA training manifold, the system defaults to conservative population‑based dosing rather than personalised AI recommendations, a principle articulated in the OmniCortex framework.

4.2 LinAge2 Clinical Biological‑Age Monitoring and Endpoint Hierarchy

LinAge2 outperforms chronological age, GrimAge, and PhenoAge for 10‑ and 20‑year mortality prediction and provides organ‑system‑specific outputs (NUS Medicine, 2025). The protocol organises outcome assessment into a hierarchical structure that prevents composite‑outcome bias and allows each intervention layer to be evaluated independently:

· Primary efficacy endpoint: LinAge2 biological age reduction ≥3 years at Week 20.
· Secondary endpoints (Phase 1): IL‑6, TNF‑α, IL‑8 normalisation to young‑adult ranges; TREC count ≥1,000/μL; naïve CD4⁺ T‑cell percentage ≥15%; ≥30% reduction in p16⁺ senescent cell burden.
· Exploratory endpoints (Phase 1): AURORA‑reconstructed organ‑specific clocks; circulating SASP factors; T‑cell receptor repertoire diversity.
· Functional endpoints (Phase 2): Grip strength, VO₂ max, Montreal Cognitive Assessment, gait speed, Fried frailty index.

4.3 ClockBase Agent for Candidate Discovery

The ClockBase Agent (Stanford–Harvard–Broad Institute, 2025) autonomously analysed ~2 million molecular profiles and identified over 500 interventions that reduce biological age. To translate this resource into clinical action, the MLBRS framework operationalises a ClockBase Prioritisation Matrix that ranks candidates by: (i) mechanism alignment with MLBRS hallmarks, (ii) tissue tropism, (iii) existing human safety data, and (iv) predicted drug–drug interactions with the Phase 1 core components. This matrix converts a massive discovery dataset into an actionable, transparent pre‑screen.

4.4 Counterfactual Dosing Optimiser

Accounting for chronobiology, pharmacogenomic variants (CYP450 polymorphisms), and sex‑dimorphic ageing trajectories, the optimiser recommends not merely which interventions to deploy but when and at what dose. A human‑in‑the‑loop governance structure is codified: an independent clinician board reviews every AI‑generated recommendation bi‑weekly, documents acceptance or rejection with rationale, and compares forecasted vs. actual outcomes, creating a continuous learning loop that improves counterfactual accuracy over time.

