6. Polypharmacy Integration and Cohort Generalisability

Older multimorbid adults with polypharmacy (≥5 daily medications) are severely under‑represented in clinical trials yet constitute the population most likely to benefit from gerotherapeutic intervention. The protocol implements two safeguards:

· STOPP/START v3 medication review at enrolment, linked to computational drug‑interaction databases (DrugBank, PharmGKB).
· Polypharmacy interaction matrix that stratifies participants and pre‑defines dose adjustments. For example, participants on strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole) are assigned to 50% mRNA‑LNP dose reduction; those on immunosuppressive corticosteroids are excluded from CAR‑T cohorts. This approach directly builds on evidence from the SPPiRE and OPTIMATE trials.

---

7. PROTAC Clinical Readiness: Monitored Readiness with Explicit Triggers

PROTAC‑based senolytics (SenoTACs) offer theoretical advantages—reduced platelet toxicity, degradation of undruggable SCAP proteins—but no PROTAC‑based senolytic has entered clinical trials. The MLBRS framework establishes a formal PROTAC Clinical Readiness Assessment: a quarterly systematic review of the PROTAC pipeline with pre‑specified GO/NO‑GO criteria. GO requires at least one PROTAC‑based senolytic in Phase 1 human trials, ≥30% senescent cell clearance ex vivo, and no thrombocytopenia >Grade 1. If GO criteria are met, the PROTAC candidate undergoes organoid antagonism screening before human integration. If NO‑GO criteria persist through Year 3, the CAR‑T senolysis track proceeds as the definitive strategy.

