10. Ethical Framework and Informed Consent

Given the first‑in‑human, multi‑layer nature of the protocol, a dedicated ethical framework is embedded. The informed consent document explicitly:

· Describes the iCasp9 suicide‑switch as “an experimental kill switch that can eliminate engineered cells, tested in cancer trials but not in healthy ageing research”;
· States that partial epigenetic reprogramming carries a teratoma risk and that “we will image your whole body regularly to look for any abnormal growths”;
· Clarifies that “this is a research study, not a proven treatment; participation does not guarantee any health benefit”;
· Distinguishes between reversible interventions (butyrate, transient mRNA) and potentially irreversible epigenetic changes from OSK reprogramming.

An independent Data Safety Monitoring Board (DSMB) with expertise in geroscience, CAR‑T therapy, gene therapy, bioethics, and biostatistics will review safety data monthly during Phase 1 and quarterly during Phase 2, with authority to recommend modification or termination.

