## Appendix I: Methodological Justifications for Key Quantitative Thresholds

*This appendix addresses four specific methodological gaps identified during peer review of MLBRS v3.0, providing evidence-based justification for quantitative thresholds, biopsy protocols, reference population sources, and out-of-distribution detection calibration.*

---

### I.1 Justification for the LinAge2 ≥3-Year Biological Age Reduction Threshold

**The Problem**

Section 3.2.1 specifies a LinAge2 biological age reduction of ≥3 years as a primary entry criterion for Phase 2. Without grounding in mortality curve data, this threshold is vulnerable to the criticism of being arbitrarily chosen.

**Evidence Base**

The 3-year threshold is derived from three converging lines of evidence:

**(a) Mortality inflection analysis.** Longitudinal analyses of biological age clocks consistently demonstrate that a 2–4 year reduction in epigenetic age is associated with a statistically significant decrease in all-cause mortality risk. Specifically, Levine et al. (2018) demonstrated that each 1-year reduction in PhenoAge relative to chronological age corresponds to a ~4–6% reduction in mortality hazard in UK Biobank participants. The LinAge2 validation cohort (NUS Medicine, 2025) replicated this relationship with stronger effect sizes for 10- and 20-year mortality endpoints. A ≥3-year reduction therefore represents the lower bound of the clinically meaningful mortality-relevant effect range.

**(b) Intervention precedent.** The most rigorously documented biological age reversal in humans — the TRIIM trial (Fahy et al., 2019) using growth hormone, DHEA, and metformin — produced a mean epigenetic age reversal of 2.5 years (Horvath clock) over 12 months. A ≥3-year threshold for a substantially more intensive multimodal intervention over a 20-week Phase 1 is therefore ambitious but physiologically plausible, and not so stringent as to be functionally unachievable.

**(c) Sensitivity analysis.** To pre-register the robustness of this threshold, the protocol specifies a sensitivity analysis at two alternative cutoffs — ≥2 years (permissive) and ≥4 years (stringent) — applied post-hoc to the Phase 1 dataset. This allows the DSMB to evaluate whether the ≥3-year criterion remains the optimal Phase 2 gate without pre-specifying a threshold that may prove systematically unachievable in a geriatric population.

**Conclusion.** The ≥3-year threshold is evidence-anchored, physiologically calibrated against the best available human rejuvenation data, and protected by a pre-registered sensitivity analysis. It is not arbitrary.

