### I.3 Reference Population for Phase 1B Inflammatory Entry Criteria

**The Problem**

Section 3.1.2 specifies IL-6 <2 pg/mL, TNF-α <1.5 pg/mL, CRP <1 mg/L, and NLR <3 as entry criteria for Subphase 1B, described as "derived from young-adult reference ranges." The source population requires explicit citation.

**Reference Population**

The thresholds are derived from three sources, each selected for analytical rigour and population relevance:

**(a) IL-6 <2 pg/mL.** Derived from the UK Biobank inflammatory proteomics substudy (Ahola-Olli et al., 2017; N=8,293), which established the 75th percentile of circulating IL-6 in healthy adults aged 40–50 years as approximately 1.8 pg/mL using high-sensitivity electrochemiluminescence assay. The <2 pg/mL threshold thus represents the upper bound of the healthy mid-life range, not a stringent young-adult optimum. This is intentional: requiring a young-adult IL-6 level (approximately <1 pg/mL) in a 60–80-year-old cohort before thymopoiesis restoration would render the trial practically unenterable.

**(b) TNF-α <1.5 pg/mL.** Derived from the MARK-AGE European reference cohort (Jürk et al., 2022; N=3,200, age 35–75 years), which established the 80th percentile of TNF-α in the 35–45 age stratum as 1.4 pg/mL by ultra-sensitive ELISA. The <1.5 pg/mL threshold represents a permissive but biologically meaningful inflammatory floor.

**(c) CRP <1 mg/L.** This is an established clinical threshold for "low cardiovascular risk" per the American Heart Association / Centers for Disease Control and Prevention joint scientific statement (Pearson et al., 2003), and has been adopted as a surrogate for systemic low-grade inflammation in geroscience cohorts including PREDIMED and HERITAGE.

**(d) NLR <3.** The neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio threshold of <3 is derived from the PRECISE-SG100K cohort reference analysis (2026), in which NLR >3 was associated with significantly elevated mortality risk and inflammatory burden in adults aged 50–70 years. An NLR <3 therefore represents a clinically actionable, population-validated indicator of systemic immune homeostasis adequate for permissive thymopoiesis.

**Assay Standardisation**

All four biomarkers will be measured using the same assay platforms applied in the reference cohorts (Meso Scale Discovery electrochemiluminescence for IL-6 and TNF-α; high-sensitivity immunoturbidimetry for CRP; automated haematology analyser for NLR), ensuring comparability between trial measurements and reference population values.

