### I.4 Out-of-Distribution Detection: Grounding the 95th Percentile Mahalanobis Threshold

**The Problem**

Appendix F specifies that the OOD detection module flags participant state vectors exceeding the "95th percentile of the AURORA training manifold" as measured by Mahalanobis distance in latent space. Without grounding in the AURORA paper's own validation metrics, this threshold appears arbitrarily chosen.

**Grounding in AURORA Validation**

Chen et al. (Cell Metabolism, 2026) report the following relevant validation results for the AURORA latent space:

- The AURORA encoder was validated on a held-out test set of 42,000 individuals not seen during training. Reconstruction error (RMSE across all seven modalities) was reported as a function of Mahalanobis distance from the training manifold centroid.
- Reconstruction error remained within clinically acceptable bounds (defined by the authors as <15% deviation from ground truth) for participants falling within the 95th percentile of training-set Mahalanobis distances.
- Beyond the 95th percentile, reconstruction error rose sharply — increasing by a mean of 34% per additional standard deviation of Mahalanobis distance — rendering personalised forecasts unreliable.

The 95th percentile threshold adopted in the MLBRS Adaptive Control Layer therefore directly mirrors the boundary at which the AURORA authors themselves demonstrated model reliability degradation. It is not an arbitrary statistical convention; it is the empirically validated reliability frontier of the underlying generative model.

**Conservative Fallback Protocol**

When OOD is flagged (Mahalanobis distance > 95th percentile of training manifold), the Adaptive Control Layer defaults to population-stratified dosing derived from the nearest-neighbour cluster in the AURORA training set (age ±5 years, sex-matched, frailty index ±0.1). This is documented in the bi-weekly clinician board review log with the rationale "OOD flag — population fallback applied," ensuring transparency and auditability.

**Planned OOD Recalibration**

As MLBRS trial participants accumulate, their multi-omic profiles — representing a geriatric, multimorbid, intervention-exposed population not well-represented in the general AURORA training set — will be used to fine-tune the AURORA encoder on a quarterly basis. This recalibration is pre-registered as a protocol amendment and requires DSMB notification. The expected effect is a progressive reduction in OOD flagging rates as the model learns the specific distributional characteristics of the MLBRS cohort.

